course: dictionary
lesson: 11151
title: Wildly: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word wildly can mean wild movement, very much, or growing freely. «Sodium fizzes wildly in water» shows movement that is fast and out of control.
explain: Look at the words near wildly. In «the ferrets rush wildly for the fireplace», rush tells you the movement is fast and uncontrolled. In «Doubling grows wildly: fold paper and the layers go 2, 4, 8, 16», wildly means extremely fast, not out of control.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. «My guess was wildly wrong» means «my guess was very wrong», and that makes sense. If your meaning does not fit, try the next one. This is how you pick the right meaning from context.
fact: Wildly is an adverb: it tells you how something is done, like «cheer wildly» or «wave wildly».
fact: Wildly means in a wild, uncontrolled way, with lots of excited or fierce movement.
fact: Wildly can also mean very much or extremely, as in «wildly popular» and «wildly wrong».
fact: A wildly growing plant grows freely, without anyone looking after it.
fact: Check the other words in the sentence to find the meaning: «wildly different» means very different, and «temperatures changed wildly» means they changed by a lot.
exercise: «The crowd cheered wildly when the goal went in.» What does wildly show? | *Excited, uncontrolled cheering | Quiet and calm | Sadly | Slowly and carefully
exercise: Sam says, «My guess was wildly wrong.» What does wildly mean here? | Quietly | In a wild, uncontrolled way | Slightly | *Very much, extremely wrong
exercise: «The plant grew wildly in the garden, with nobody to look after it.» What does wildly mean here? | Right on time | Beautifully arranged | Very angrily | *Freely, without anyone looking after it
test: «Her heart beat wildly as she opened the letter.» What does wildly show? | Sleepy | *Strong, uncontrolled feeling | Calm and slow | Very small
test: «Temperatures changed wildly during the experiment.» What does wildly mean here? | Changed a tiny bit | Changed in a neat, slow way | *Changed by very large amounts | Stayed the same
exam: Which phrase uses wildly to mean very much or extremely? | Wave wildly | Cheer wildly | *Wildly different | Barking wildly
